WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.492
A hunt hypothesis is a focused,

00:00:02.492 --> 00:00:06.876
testable statement about activity that may be present in the environment.

00:00:06.876 --> 00:00:11.659
It gives the hunt direction and defines what evidence would support, weaken,

00:00:11.659 --> 00:00:13.254
or disprove the idea.

00:00:13.254 --> 00:00:18.834
A vague goal such as finding anything suspicious produces unfocused searches and uncertain conclusions.

00:00:18.834 --> 00:00:21.625
Threat-informed hypotheses can begin with relevant intelligence,

00:00:21.625 --> 00:00:26.009
but they must be adapted to the actual architecture and assets.

00:00:26.009 --> 00:00:29.198
Incident lessons, known control weaknesses, vulnerabilities, change history,

00:00:29.198 --> 00:00:32.387
and operator concerns can also identify useful questions.

00:00:32.387 --> 00:00:38.366
A remote-access hypothesis might ask whether vendor sessions reached assets outside approved destinations or times.

00:00:38.366 --> 00:00:43.149
An engineering hypothesis might ask whether a privileged workstation communicated with a

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:45.541
controller without a matching work order.

00:00:45.541 --> 00:00:51.520
A pathway hypothesis might ask whether systems crossed a zone boundary through an undocumented route.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.303
Write the hypothesis with scope, time range, assets, expected behavior, data sources,

00:00:56.303 --> 00:00:57.897
exclusions, and required reviewers.

00:00:57.897 --> 00:00:59.890
Identify blind spots before searching.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:03.079
If the available telemetry cannot answer the question,

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:07.065
the hunt should say so rather than forcing a conclusion.

00:01:07.065 --> 00:01:10.653
Look for disconfirming evidence as well as supporting evidence.

00:01:10.653 --> 00:01:12.646
Planned maintenance, failover, equipment replacement,

00:01:12.646 --> 00:01:15.037
and troubleshooting may explain unusual activity.

00:01:15.037 --> 00:01:19.422
Confirmation bias is especially risky when technical patterns have process meaning.

00:01:19.422 --> 00:01:24.205
A completed hunt states the result and confidence: supported, not supported, inconclusive,

00:01:24.205 --> 00:01:25.800
or unable to test.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.786
Even a disproved hypothesis can improve baselines, inventory, telemetry coverage,

00:01:29.786 --> 00:01:31.380
and future detection design.
